A||||13||loafer87gt||Car Still Cutting out. Please help!||||1||
Z||000000||loafer87gt||04-19-2000||03:23 PM||||The head mechanic at the dealership called today and said that I might as well come and pick it up from them because they have given up on the car. What a load of utter BULLSH*T! After charging me $1300 for parts and labor that didn't fix the problem, the tow bills, and being three weeks without the car they just give up. You guys have no idea how frustrated I am. This is the only dealership that was willing to work on my car, so I guess my only other option is to haul the car 300 kilometers to my old backyard mechanic. The problem if you guys didn't see in the tech section is that the car is stumbling and stalling, the revs surge from 1000 rpm to wherever position you have the throttle depressed to. So when you have the pedal down the car goes Blat-stall-blat-stall-blat! If you drive the car for 15 minutes and shut it off, and fire it back up it does this. This really sucks, its 20+ outside today and everyone is ripping around in their sportscars and I'm still stuck on my bike, it just ain't fair.<P>So far the GM dealership has replaced the ECM, the MAP sensor, EGR sensor, plugs, plugwires, distributor, ignition module, coil, cap and rotor, and it is still doing it. I also had the injectors cleaned about a month back, and the alternator replaced last fall. They said they have checked every bit of wiring in the car but still can't seem to track down the problem.||||reg||1
Z||000001||lennyb4||04-19-2000||03:30 PM||||Could it be the throttle position sensor?||||reg||5
Z||000002||Raydar||04-19-2000||03:43 PM||||Are you getting any codes? Does the "Check Engine" light ever come on? (If you turn on the key without starting the engine, it should come on.) <BR>Did they check fuel pressure? Oil pressure sender? (It controls the fuel pump relay. If the pressure goes away, it's supposed to shut off the fuel pump.) It sounds as if you're running out of gas, then as soon as it stalls, it sounds like the pump is picking up again. <BR>This is a a total shot in the dark. Good luck. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||04-19-2000||03:51 PM||||Ooops. Could be. Should set a code if the lite is working.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||04-19-2000||04:01 PM||||I don't know what you call the equivalent of small claims court but I'd look into that.<P>Check the codes. If that doesn't tell you anything then it's harder but not imposible.<P>It could be a number of things. Many of which are annoying but not hard to find. The first to things are vacuum leak search, fuel pressure and base timing. <P>Check larger diameter vacuum lines very carefully. Clamp the big ones if they are even slightly loose. (including rubber elbows on PCV line and rubber sections of brake booster line) don't make the clamps so tight they cut into the rubber. make sure All the intake hardware is tight.<P>If you don't get codes you might want to invest in an AutoXray GM scanner. ($150us if you shop around. $200us at most I've seen.) This will let you very quickly see what sensors are doing what. (Don't confuse the GM scanner with the XP240. XP240 is the universal scanner and costs allot more.)<P>visually inspect all wiring. Anything damaged needs replaced. Pull off all connectors one at a time. Make sure thay aren't corroded.<P>Check the vacuum line to the MAP very carefully. if that line leaks or is blocked the MAP won't work right. Part of MAP's function is the calculation of ignition timing. If the MAP can't respond quickly to changes in vacuum the ECM will screw up the timing.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||GT Bastard||04-19-2000||05:01 PM||||Get yourself a vaccum guage and hook it up to the manifold.  You be able to see right away if it's leaking or not.  <P>How bout the O2 sensor?  Has it been replaced?  <P>I agree with ogre.  The fuel pressure should be checked.  You could rent or borrow a fuel pressure guage.  They're not that hard to operate, and it's a sure way of finding out if it's being starved.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||burdell1||04-19-2000||06:07 PM||||Did they replace the PROM when they replaced the ECM? If so, double check to make sure they got the right year and engine type.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fret Naught||04-19-2000||07:17 PM||||I had a problem like that with my Sons' car not a fiero, turned out the vented gas cap was plugged. I would check the charcoal canister and that portion of the emissions system. Also never had a problems with a catylitic converter but might not be a bad idea to check that. Whatever you find when it is fixed, I would go after that dealer for at least some of my money those guys are supposed to be PRO'S There is implied faith I bring my car to you give you lots of money and you fix my car, not I give you loads of money, and you don't fix anything. There incompentence is not your problem.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Fformula88||04-19-2000||07:39 PM||||A long with everything that has been mentioned, I'd check the cat.  If its partially plugged, it will cause the car to run bad from backpressure.  You just need to hook up a backpressure guage whith the car on and you will instantly know if thats the problem.  Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000009||tgowens||04-20-2000||01:54 AM||||well, here is yet another idea -- I was having a similar problem - turns out it was the engine coolant sensor - replaced it and the problem hasn't happened since!||||reg||4
Z||000010||GT Bastard||04-20-2000||02:50 AM||||There's a ton of good info here Loafer.  Don't give up.  This stuff works!  I'd be really suprised if you weren't able to fix the problem via the suggestions posted here so far. <P>Can you think of anything that could have possibly triggered the problem?  Had the car been driven hard before-hand?  Had it gotten wet?  Anything at all?<P>Also, you may want to consider the possibility that the dealer didn't actually change all the plugs.  I could definitely picture them just bypassing the rear ones because of the difficulty.  It may be worth looking into.||||reg||1
Z||000011||loafer87gt||04-20-2000||03:05 AM||||Yes, I just cleaned my engine up with water before the problem started to happen. I checked the diagnostic forms from the GM place and the car is had a code 33 and 44 in the history, and the EGR tube is cracked. I'm not too sure what the codes mean, I'll have to check. The shop technician said that these codes wouldn't be relevant to the problem. I'm picking the car up in the morning and taking back home to my acreage and getting some of my buddies to come over and see if we can figure it out. I think the problem with a lot of shops now days is that they don't bother to actually get under the hood and do some mechanic work, they jsut rely on their diagnostic tools to do all the work and troubleshooting. Wish me luck guys, I'll keep you posted. BTW, my old mechanic also suggested that it might be the fuel pressure sender unit. If this went though, wouldn't the car run like crap all the time and not just intermitently like it is doing now?||||reg||1
Z||000012||pollock||04-20-2000||10:51 AM||||On various cars, every time i've experienced a surging idle, cleaning out the throttle body did the trick.  Take it off and scrub it with a toothbrush.  There are cleaners made specifically for TBs.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||theogre||04-20-2000||11:31 AM||||44 and 33 can both be caused y the crcked EGR lines. (44 lean mix, 33 MAP measured high.)||||reg||1
